    "id": "guide-22",
    "slug": "mid-drive-electric-bikes-guide",
    "title": "Mid Drive Electric Bikes: Who Needs One and What They Cost",
    "seoTitle": "Mid Drive Electric Bikes: Who Needs One and the Cost",
    "excerpt": "Mid drive electric bikes climb better and ride more naturally. See how they work, which Bosch and Shimano bikes we stock and who should pay the extra.",
    "content": "Mid drive electric bikes put the motor at the pedals instead of in a wheel, and that one change is why they climb better, balance better and feel more like riding a normal bike. This guide explains how a mid-drive works, why riders pay extra for it and which Bosch and Shimano models in our range suit commuting, trails or touring. The short answer is simple: if you ride hills, carry loads or want the most natural feel, a mid-drive is worth the extra. If you ride short, flat trips on a budget, a hub motor is usually enough.\n\n![mid drive electric bikes: Cube Touring Hybrid ONE 600 Electric Hybrid Bike](/images/products/cube-touring-hybrid-one-600.webp#800x600)\n\n## What a mid-drive motor does\n\nA mid-drive motor sits in the frame beside the pedals and drives the chain, so its power goes through the bike gears. A hub motor, by contrast, spins the wheel directly. A mid drive ebike motor can use a low gear to climb a steep hill at high revs, which is why it handles climbs without overheating, and a sensor reads how hard you pedal and adds matching assistance. The result is smooth, proportional help instead of a push from the back wheel.\n\nIn Australia all of the e-bikes we list are limited to 250 W continuous power with assistance that cuts out at 25 km/h, which is the EN 15194 standard that Bicycle Network covers here: [Brose eBike systems](https://www.brose-ebike.com/en/).\n\nBrands use different names for the same idea. You may see a mid motor ebike or a centre motor bike described in a listing, and the meaning is identical: the motor drives the cranks rather than the wheel. An electric bicycle mid drive motor is also easier to protect, because it sits low in the frame where stones and spray reach it less than they reach a wheel.\n\n## Why mid-drive climbs better\n\nTorque and gearing are the practical difference. A mid-drive uses the gears to multiply the motor effort, so the same motor feels stronger on a 12 percent climb than a hub motor of similar power. Weight is also centred low in the frame, which helps handling on gravel and trails. For riders who face real hills every day, this is the single biggest reason to choose a mid-drive over a cheaper bike.\n\nFor trail riding the effect is bigger again, which is why almost every serious e-MTB in our [emtb australia](/ebikes/electric-mountain-bike) range uses one.\n\nSmooth power delivery is another advantage. Because the motor reads pedal force through a torque sensor, assistance rises and falls with your effort, so a mid drive e bike never surges or pushes you faster than you intend. That makes it far easier to control on gravel, in traffic and on narrow trails, and it is the main reason experienced riders prefer it.\n\n## Bosch, Shimano and other mid-drive systems in our range\n\nOur range uses several mid-drive systems. Bosch Performance Line motors power the Cube Touring Hybrid, Cube Reaction and Cube Stereo ranges, Shimano motors power Merida e-MTBs such as the eOne-Sixty with the EP801, and Pedal uses Shimano and Ananda units on bikes like the Falcon 3C and Lynx 3. Bosch and Shimano are both widely supported, so replacement parts and service are easier to find than for unknown makes. You can see the Cube line on the [cube e mountain bikes](/brands/cube) page.\n\nBrands also differ in feel. Bosch tends to feel strong and immediate when you start pedalling, while Shimano feels smooth and gradual, so a test ride shows which one suits you. Both are limited to 250 W and 25 km/h here, so the real difference is the way the power arrives, not the headline number.\n\n| Model | Brand | Price (AUD) |\n| [Cube Touring Hybrid ONE 600 Electric Hybrid Bike](/product/cube-touring-hybrid-one-600) | Cube | $3,299 |\n| [Cube Reaction Hybrid Performance 500 Electric Hardtail Mountain Bike](/product/cube-reaction-hybrid-performance-500) | Cube | $2,999 |\n| [Merida eOne-Sixty 400 Electric Enduro Bike](/product/merida-eone-sixty-400) | Merida | $4,477 |\n| [Pedal Falcon 3C Electric Hybrid Bike Satin Blue](/product/pedal-falcon-3c-electric-hybrid-bike-satin-blue) | Pedal | $2,499 |\n| [Pedal Lynx 3 Electric Hardtail Mountain Bike](/product/pedal-lynx-3-electric-hardtail-mountain-bike) | Pedal | $2,145 |\n\nPrices are the current listed prices at the time of writing and can change. Check each product page for the latest price and stock.\n\n## Mid-drive or hub: who should pay more\n\nA mid-drive costs more because the motor, sensors and frame are more complex. The extra is worth paying if you meet two or more of these tests: you ride hills most days, you carry cargo or a child seat, you want a quiet and natural feel, or you plan to keep the bike for many years. If your commute is short and flat, a hub motor on an entry hybrid is cheaper and perfectly good. Our [mid drive electric bike](/electric-hybrid-bikes) page shows how the two types compare in practice, and the [electric bike for commuters](/ebikes/electric-commuter-bikes) page shows commuter bikes with each.\n\nSearch results for a mid drive electric bike Australia buyers can rely on often mix imports with local stock, so check the warranty and ask who services the motor. Bosch and Shimano both have service networks across the country, which is a major reason to choose them over a no-name unit that nobody can repair.\n\n## Servicing and chain wear\n\nThe one trade-off is wear. Because the motor pushes through the chain, chains, cassettes and chainrings wear faster than on a hub-motor bike, so servicing costs are a little higher. Shift gently, ease off the pedals while changing gear and keep the chain clean and lubricated. A quality chain lasts several thousand kilometres when cared for. Genuine Bosch parts are listed on our [bosch ebike parts](/e-bike-parts) page, which is useful because mid-drive systems expect matched components.\n\nBudget for a yearly service and replace the chain before it stretches, because a stretched chain wears the cassette and chainring faster than a new one. A simple routine like this keeps a mid-drive bike running smoothly for tens of thousands of kilometres.\n\n## Mid-drive e-bikes compared\n\nHere are examples from our range, from entry level to trail. Prices are current listed prices and change from time to time, so check each page before you buy. Every bike below uses a mid-drive motor, and the embedded pick, the [Cube Touring Hybrid ONE 600 Electric Hybrid Bike](/product/cube-touring-hybrid-one-600), is a good all-rounder for mixed commuting.\n\n## How to choose your first mid-drive\n\nStart with your ride. If hills and loads dominate, choose a mid-drive and aim for a 500 Wh battery or more. If you want the lowest price, check whether a hub bike meets your needs. Test ride one of each if you can, because the feel is the difference people mention most. A mid drive electric bike in Australia is usually bought once and ridden for years, so it pays to buy the one that suits your hills. For more on the technical side, see our motor guide: [Electric bicycle (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_bicycle).\n\nFinally, check the battery capacity, the display and the charger. A bigger battery does not make a bike climb better, but it does make long hill days possible, and a clear display helps you manage assistance on long rides. Ask to see the bike with the battery fitted, so you feel the real weight.\n\nMid drive electric bikes are the best choice for hills, loads and long-term riding. They cost more and wear chains faster, but the natural feel and climbing ability are hard to beat. Compare a few models in the table, check the battery size and book a test ride before you decide.",
