    "id": "guide-28",
    "slug": "electric-bike-repair-guide-australia",
    "title": "Electric Bike Repair: What You Can Fix and When to Call a Pro",
    "seoTitle": "Electric Bike Repair: What You Can Fix and When to Call",
    "excerpt": "Electric bike repair starts with simple checks you can do at home. Learn common faults, basic maintenance and when to book a qualified mechanic.",
    "content": "Electric bike repair is mostly the same as normal bike repair, plus a few extra checks on the battery, the connectors and the motor. Many problems are easy to fix at home: a flat tyre, a loose brake or a dirty chain. Others, such as a motor fault or a damaged battery, need a qualified mechanic. This guide lists the most common faults, the checks you can do yourself and the signs that tell you it is time to book a service. It also lists the parts that wear out first, so you can plan for them before they stop you riding.\n\n![electric bike repair: Pedal Comet 3 Disc Electric Hybrid Bike Charcoal](/images/products/pedal-comet-3-disc-electric-hybrid-bike-charcoal.webp#800x600)\n\n## Common faults and what they mean\n\nMost faults fall into a handful of groups. The bike will not turn on, which is usually a flat battery, a loose connector or a switch left off. Assistance cuts out, which can be a sensor, a connector or a low battery. Brakes squeal or feel weak, which is normally worn pads or a dirty rotor. The chain skips, which is wear or a bent hanger. And the tyre loses air, which is a puncture or a worn valve. Knowing which group you are in tells you whether to fix it or book a pro.\n\nWrite the fault down as precisely as you can, including when it happens, such as only on hills or only when cold. A clear description saves time and money at the workshop.\n\n## Quick checks you can do at home\n\nStart with a simple routine before you ride. Check the tyre pressure, squeeze both brakes, look at the chain and make sure the battery is seated and locked. If the bike will not start, fully charge the battery, check that the connectors are clean and dry and try a different power point for the charger. These steps solve more than half of the problems riders report, and they take a few minutes. Keep a note of the date so you can tell the mechanic if the same fault returns. Our [best electric bike australia](/ebikes) range lists bikes that come with clear manuals.\n\nMany ebike repairs begin with a visual check. Look for loose bolts, cracked cables, damaged connectors and a bent derailleur hanger, and listen for rubbing or clicking when you spin the pedals. Most issues are visible if you look slowly, and a photo helps when you call the workshop. Keep your phone handy to record any noise or error code on the display.\n\n## Tyres, brakes and chain care\n\nTyres, brakes and the chain wear the fastest. Keep tyres at the pressure printed on the sidewall, replace brake pads before they are thin and clean and lubricate the chain every few hundred kilometres. E-bikes are heavier than normal bikes and travel faster, so pads and tyres wear sooner. If you repair a puncture yourself, check the inside of the tyre for the sharp object, because it can puncture the new tube. For parts, see our [electric bike parts](/e-bike-parts) page, which lists tyres, brake pads and other wear items. Always check the part fits your model before you order.\n\nE bike repairs often involve parts that are shared with ordinary bikes, which keeps costs down. Brake pads, tyres, tubes, chains and cables are standard items that any good bike shop can fit. The parts that are specific to e-bikes, such as the motor, display and battery, are covered by the maker or seller under warranty during the first two years.\n\n## Battery and charger problems\n\nBattery and charger problems need care. Never open a battery pack yourself, never use a charger that is not made for your battery and stop using a battery that is swollen, hot, leaking or damaged. If the bike loses range quickly, the battery may be ageing, and a replacement is the fix. The ACCC Product Safety site has clear advice on e-bike and battery safety: [ACCC Product Safety: e-bikes and e-micromobility devices](https://www.productsafety.gov.au/business/e-bikes-e-scooters-and-other-e-micromobility-devices). For details on replacing or buying a battery, see our [ebike battery](/electric-bike-batteries) page.\n\nIf you decide to repair ebike parts yourself, use the right tools, a clean workspace and the maker manual. Torque wrenches matter on carbon and aluminium parts, and thread-lock helps on bolts that vibrate loose. Disconnect the battery before you touch wiring, and never force a connector.\n\n## When to see a qualified mechanic\n\nBook a qualified mechanic when the fault involves the motor, the controller, the wiring or the battery management system, or when you are not sure what is wrong. A mechanic has diagnostic tools, the right software and the training to work with high-voltage components safely. Ask whether the shop is trained on your brand, because systems from Bosch and Shimano each have their own tools. A good shop will give you a written quote before work begins.\n\nA good e bike service includes a full check of brakes, tyres, drivetrain, bolts, lights and battery connections, and a software check where the system supports it.\n\n## Parts that wear out first\n\nThese parts usually wear out first and are worth keeping in mind. They are listed below with approximate prices from our range, so you can plan your budget. A set of disc brake pads, a floor pump, a tyre and a kickstand are the most common purchases, and all are cheap compared with a lost weekend of riding. Check that each part suits your bike, and ask us if you are unsure. The [Pedal Comet 3 Disc Electric Hybrid Bike Charcoal](/product/pedal-comet-3-disc-electric-hybrid-bike-charcoal) is a good example of a bike that is easy to maintain.\n\nE-bike repairs are cheaper when you catch them early. A worn chain costs a few dollars to replace, but a worn chain left alone damages the cassette and chainring, which costs much more. A squealing brake costs a pair of pads, but a worn rotor costs more. Set reminders for the checks that matter.\n\n| Model | Brand | Price (AUD) |\n| [Shimano BR-MT400 B05S Resin Disc Brake Pads & Spring](/product/shimano-br-mt400-b05s-resin-disc-brake-pads-spring) | Shimano | $19 |\n| [Shimano Altus MT200 Rear Disc Brake with Left Lever](/product/shimano-altus-mt200-rear-disc-brake-with-left-lever) | Shimano | $54 |\n| [JetBlack Good Fella 2.0 Top Mounted Gauge Steel Floor Pump w/ Smart Head](/product/jetblack-good-fella-2-0-top-mounted-gauge-steel-floor-pump-w-smart-head) | Jetblack | $62 |\n| [Continental GP5000 Folding Road Tyre](/product/continental-gp5000-folding-road-tyre) | Continental | $90 |\n| [QBP Rear Mount Adjustable Kickstand 24-29'' (Disc Brake Compatible)](/product/qbp-rear-mount-adjustable-kickstand-24-29-disc-brake-compatible) | QBP | $32 |\n\nPrices are the current listed prices at the time of writing and can change. Check each product page for the latest price and stock.\n\n## Keeping a service record\n\nKeep a service record. Note the date, the work done and the distance, and keep receipts for parts and labour. This helps with warranty claims, makes the bike easier to sell and tells you when the next service is due. A simple note on your phone is enough. A yearly service is a good habit, with a quick check every few months if you ride often. Our [ebike accessories](/e-bike-accessories) page lists the accessories that make home maintenance easier, and the [ebike conversion kit](/electric-bike-conversion-kits) page lists conversion kits if you are curious about how motors work.\n\nFor more on how e-bikes are built and why they need these extra checks, the Wikipedia entry on the electric bicycle is a helpful overview: [Electric bicycle (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_bicycle).\n\nElectric bike repair does not need to be scary. Do the quick checks at home, replace wear parts before they fail and leave the battery, motor and wiring to a qualified mechanic. A little regular care keeps your e-bike safe, reliable and ready when you are.",
