    "id": "guide-39",
    "slug": "ebike-mods-legal-guide",
    "title": "Ebike Mods: What Is Legal, What Is Safe and What Voids Warranty",
    "seoTitle": "Ebike Mods: What Is Legal, Safe and Warranty-Friendly",
    "excerpt": "Ebike mods can make a bike more comfortable and useful, but some break the law or void your warranty. See which upgrades are safe and which to avoid.",
    "content": "Ebike mods range from simple and sensible, such as better grips and lights, to risky and illegal, such as removing a speed limiter. Which ones are fine depends on three things: the law, your warranty and safety. This guide sorts common upgrades into safe, careful and avoid, explains how modifications can affect road legality and insurance, and shows which accessories improve a bike without crossing a line. If you want to personalise a bike, the best mods are usually the ones that make it more comfortable, more visible and easier to carry things.\n\n![ebike mods: Pedal Falcon 3C Electric Hybrid Bike Satin Blue](/images/products/pedal-falcon-3c-electric-hybrid-bike-satin-blue.webp#800x600)\n\n## Why riders modify e-bikes\n\nRiders modify bikes for many reasons: comfort, style, carrying capacity, safety or performance. Some people want a bike that looks unique, and custom ebikes can be as much about colour and details as performance. Others want more speed or more power, which is where the legal and safety risks lie. Think about the reason before you spend money, because a good accessory can solve a comfort problem for a few dollars while a risky mod can create a legal and warranty problem for much more.\n\nA few simple questions help: does this change make me safer, does it keep the bike legal and will the warranty still apply? If the answer to any of them is no, think again.\n\n## Mods that are safe and legal\n\nSafe and legal mods improve comfort and usefulness without changing the motor or the speed limit. Examples include better saddles, grips, pedals, mudguards, racks and baskets, lights and mirrors, a bell, a kickstand and a good lock. These are all ordinary bike parts. Our [ebike lights](/e-bike-accessories) page lists lights and other accessories, and the changes make a bike safer and more practical. A well-chosen ebike bar upgrade, such as a wider handlebar or a more comfortable shape, can also improve control, as long as the cables and brakes still fit.\n\nIf you are tempted to modify electric bike parts beyond accessories, ask yourself who carries the risk. If the bike fails, the maker and seller will point to the modification, and you may find that insurance, warranty and the law are all against you. A safe mod is one you are happy to explain to a police officer, an insurer and a seller.\n\n## Mods that break the law\n\nMods that break the law are those that change the motor power, remove the speed limit or add a throttle that drives the bike above walking speed. A bike that exceeds the Australian e-bike limits of 250 W and 25 km/h assistance is no longer treated as a bicycle, and may need registration and a licence. The ACCC explains the safety rules for e-bikes and batteries: [ACCC Product Safety: e-bikes and e-micromobility devices](https://www.productsafety.gov.au/business/e-bikes-e-scooters-and-other-e-micromobility-devices). Do not buy tuning dongles or controllers that unlock speed, and do not modify the motor or controller to increase power.\n\nSearch results for how to modify ebike speed or power are full of videos that ignore the law. Treat them with caution, because the rider who suffers the consequences is you, not the person who made the video.\n\n## Warranty and insurance effects\n\nWarranty and insurance are the hidden costs. Most warranties are void if you change the motor, the controller, the battery or the wiring, and some are void if you fit parts that were not approved. An insurer may refuse a claim if a modified bike is involved in a crash. If you want to modify an electric bike in any way beyond simple accessories, ask the seller first and get the answer in writing. The Bicycle Network guide to the EN 15194 standard explains why the limits exist: [Bicycle Network: federal return to the EN 15194 e-bike standard](https://bicyclenetwork.com.au/newsroom/2025/12/03/feds-return-to-euro-e-bike-standard/).\n\nCosmetic custom ebikes are a different matter. Paint, wraps, decals, coloured cables and new grips change the look without changing the performance. If you want a bike that is unique, start there, and keep a record of what you changed in case you sell the bike later.\n\n## Batteries and motors: leave to the pros\n\nBatteries and motors are best left to the professionals. A modified electric bicycle with a bigger or non-standard battery can overheat, damage the controller or start a fire. If you need a replacement or an extra battery, buy one that is made for your bike, with the right voltage and connector, from the maker or an approved seller. Our [48v ebike battery](/electric-bike-batteries) page lists battery options, and our [converter e bike](/electric-bike-conversion-kits) page lists conversion kits for riders who want to add a motor to a normal bike within the rules.\n\nAnother useful mod is a better set of tyres. A tyre with good puncture protection and a suitable tread can transform comfort and grip, and it costs far less than a motor upgrade. Check the maximum tyre width for the frame and the fork, and match the pressure to your weight.\n\n## Upgrades worth making\n\nThe table lists accessories that make a good starting point, with current prices from our range. A bright rear light, a good pump, a front basket, a strong lock and a rear basket are cheap, useful and legal. The embedded pick, the [Pedal Falcon 3C Electric Hybrid Bike Satin Blue](/product/pedal-falcon-3c-electric-hybrid-bike-satin-blue), is a good base bike to build on, with a Shimano motor and a mid-size battery. Prices change, so check each product page before you order. A good lock is the first upgrade we suggest, because a stolen bike is the worst outcome of all.\n\nOur accessory range is a good place to start. A set of lights, a good pump and a strong lock cost little and last years. A basket or rack adds practical carrying, and a bell and mirror add safety.\n\n| Model | Brand | Price (AUD) |\n| [JetBlack Good Fella 2.0 Top Mounted Gauge Steel Floor Pump w/ Smart Head](/product/jetblack-good-fella-2-0-top-mounted-gauge-steel-floor-pump-w-smart-head) | Jetblack | $62 |\n| [Azur Leo 50 Lumen Rear Light](/product/azur-leo-50-lumen-rear-light) | Azur | $44 |\n| [Topeak Basket Front [with Fixer 3E] (Black)](/product/topeak-basket-front-with-fixer-3e-black) | Topeak | $67 |\n| [Magnum X2P U-Lock + Cable](/product/magnum-x2p-u-lock-cable) | Magnum | $39 |\n| [Bikecorp Rear Mesh Basket](/product/bikecorp-rear-mesh-basket) | Bikecorp | $43 |\n\nPrices are the current listed prices at the time of writing and can change. Check each product page for the latest price and stock.\n\n## How to mod with confidence\n\nTo mod with confidence, follow a few rules. Keep the bike within the legal limits. Use parts made for your bike and your model. Keep the original parts so you can restore the bike. Test brakes and lights after any change. Record what you changed and when. If you want performance, choose a bike that already has it, such as a higher-torque mid-drive, rather than modifying a weaker one. Our [e bike controller](/e-bike-parts) page lists parts and controllers that suit common bikes, and the [electric assist bike](/electric-hybrid-bikes) page lists pedal-assist bikes with good power.\n\nIf you do want to learn how e-bikes work in more detail, a conversion kit on an old bike is a legal project if the kit is within the limits, and it gives you the satisfaction of building something yourself.\n\nEbike mods can be fun and useful when they stay within the law and the warranty. Choose comfort, visibility and carrying capacity over power, buy parts made for your bike and ask the seller before you change anything electrical.",
    "category": "Legal",
    "readTime": "8 min read",
